markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Displaying Objects as Cards

#### Many apps display information as cards that combine text, images, and ratings into something users can quickly scan. But real-world data is often messy. Some fields may be missing, incomplete, or even **null.**

#### When this happens, programmers and designers must decide what to show instead and those choices can influence how users understand and trust the information they see.

---
## Why Websites Do This

#### Below are two versions of the same card. One includes a missing value.

#### Websites don’t usually leave empty or broken-looking fields on the screen. Instead, they often substitute a default value so the interface still feels complete and usable even when the data is imperfect.

<img src="https://images.code.org/822e82632e65af46a8c2aca29c92adf2-image-1768937250577.png" alt="2 cards with names, titles, emails, but one is missing the image" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">


<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 A <strong>fallback value</strong> is a default value shown when data is missing or null.</p>
</div>


--- 
## Design Thinking: User Perception
#### The way information is displayed doesn’t just inform users—it can shape their opinions.

#### Below are three versions of the same card, each presenting information slightly differently.

<img src="https://images.code.org/b38f316ba798d349ef86af689fbfb366-image-1768937551031.png" alt="3 cards with names, ratings, and reviews" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

 #### 👉 **How does each version of the card influence your perception?**



<div style="clear:both;"></div>



<details style="background-color: #ececec; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px; border: 1px solid transparent; border-radius: 5px;">
    <summary style="font-size: 1.5em; font-weight: bold;">💡 Check your thinking:</summary>
<div style="margin-top: 20px; margin-left: 20px; margin-right: 20px; margin-bottom:75px;">
    <p style = "font-size: 14pt;">
Students should recognize the differences between the three versions:<br><br>
<b>Version 1:</b> Neutral
Shows a plain rating (for example, “Rating: 2.5/5 stars”).<br>
<b>Version 2:</b> Biased
Uses hype language (for example, “Top Value Pick!”).<br>
<b>Version 3:</b> Hidden Information
Leaves the rating off entirely.<br><br>
Tone, label choice (like “Top Value Pick!”), and hiding information (such as omitting a low rating) can all influence user perception and bias how trustworthy or appealing something appears.

</p>
</div>
</details>


---



